WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
GraceBench evaluates GUI-agent execution stability: 169 tasks, 10 apps, 11 models, and 3,718 evaluated trajectories. 3,042 have local video evidence.

00:08.000 --> 00:14.000
Same task: open a Discover note, visit its author, open the first author note, and stay there. Both executions reach the goal.

00:14.000 --> 00:23.000
The stable run stops after 3 actions. The unstable run revisits the author and reopens the same note six times, using all 15 actions. Action-centered cuts; variable speed.

00:23.000 --> 00:27.000
Original-speed replay, action 4: tapping the author entry leaves the required terminal screen. This is a deviation, not a Back-button action.

00:27.000 --> 00:31.000
Action 5 reopens the same target note, recovering in one action. The complete trace repeats this cycle six times without further progress.

00:31.000 --> 00:35.000
Match the task-specific subgraph and align action-centered video evidence to state–action–state transitions.

00:35.000 --> 00:39.000
S3 to S2 is a deviation: the agent leaves the required goal screen by tapping the author.

00:39.000 --> 00:43.000
S2 to S3 is an on-path return closing a recovery episode. Actions 4 through 15 form one no-progress loop event: six cycles, twelve covered actions.

00:43.000 --> 00:48.000
Both runs succeed and cover 3 of 3 required transitions. Unstable deviation rate is 6/15, or 40%; loop burden is 12/15, or 80%.

00:48.000 --> 00:53.000
Six one-action recoveries give recovery ability 1.00. The stable run has no loop or recovery opportunity: conditional metrics are not applicable. High recovery ability does not imply low loop burden.

00:53.000 --> 01:05.000
Aggregate detailed-instruction results across 11 models: success, deviation, and recovery expose different behavior. GPT-5.6 Sol has the highest success rate but lowest recovery ability on observed opportunities. Source: manuscript Table 2.
